==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|strac||straight}}, from {{inh|de|goh|*strac}} (attested in {{m|goh|framstrac}}), from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*strakkaz||straight}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*streg-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*treg-||stiff, rigid}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|strak}}. More at {{m|en|stretch}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ʃtʁak/}}
* {{audio|de|De-strack.ogg|Audio}}
* {{rhymes|de|ak|s=1}}

===Adjective===
{{de-adj|comp}}

# {{lb|de|archaic|or|dialectal}} [[straight]], [[taut]]
#: {{syn|de|steif|straff|stramm}}
# {{lb|de|colloquial}} [[drunk]]

====Usage notes====
* Now chiefly used in the figurative sense “drunk” or in the adverbial form {{m|de|stracks|t=directly}}. The literal sense is quite rare but still widely understood. It is commonest referring to people’s posture, chiefly with the verbs {{m|de|liegen}}, {{m|de|sitzen}}, {{m|de|stehen}}, sometimes also in the combination {{m|de|strack und steif}}.

====Declension====
{{de-adecl|comp}}

